Unlike in two dimensions, the angular acceleration in three dimensions need not be associated with a change in the angular ''speed __MATH_23__'': If the particle's position vector "twists" in space, changing its instantaneous plane of angular displacement, the change in the ''direction'' of the angular velocity __MATH_24__ will still produce a nonzero angular acceleration. This cannot not happen if the position vector is restricted to a fixed plane, in which case __MATH_25__ has a fixed direction perpendicular to the plane.

The angular acceleration vector is more properly called a [[pseudovector]]: It has three components which transform under rotations in the same way as the Cartesian coordinates of a point do, but which do not transform like Cartesian coordinates under reflections.

